An :EXITCASE directly after a :RETURN in the same branch is unreachable:
exitcase_after_return fires on the :EXITCASE (hint). The :EXITCASE is
still the last statement of its branch, so exitcase_last_in_branch must
not fire (diag.exitcase_after_return, diag.exitcase_last_in_branch).
-- input.ssl --
:PROCEDURE PriorityName;
	:PARAMETERS nLevel;
	:BEGINCASE;
	:CASE nLevel > 8;
		:RETURN "critical";
		:EXITCASE;
	:CASE nLevel > 4;
		:RETURN "elevated";
		/* reached only by the return above;
		:EXITCASE;
	:OTHERWISE;
		:RETURN "normal";
	:ENDCASE;
:ENDPROC;
-- format --
:PROCEDURE PriorityName;
	:PARAMETERS nLevel;

	:BEGINCASE;
	:CASE nLevel > 8;
		:RETURN "critical";
		:EXITCASE;
	:CASE nLevel > 4;
		:RETURN "elevated";

		/* reached only by the return above;
		:EXITCASE;
	:OTHERWISE;
		:RETURN "normal";
	:ENDCASE;
:ENDPROC;
-- diagnostics --
6:3-6:12 hint exitcase_after_return: Unreachable ':EXITCASE' - the preceding ':RETURN' already leaves the procedure
10:3-10:12 hint exitcase_after_return: Unreachable ':EXITCASE' - the preceding ':RETURN' already leaves the procedure
-- diagnostics [--strict --info] --
6:3-6:12 hint exitcase_after_return: Unreachable ':EXITCASE' - the preceding ':RETURN' already leaves the procedure
10:3-10:12 hint exitcase_after_return: Unreachable ':EXITCASE' - the preceding ':RETURN' already leaves the procedure
